{#
  Case studies panel — the [[casestudies]] discovery cards inside a Jonson answer.
  `caseStudies` is the list from FindContext::caseStudies() (maps: url, title,
  client, summary, skills, logo, [image]). Rendered by the panel registry when the
  model places the marker. Full-bleeds like the other Jonson panels.

  Two presentations of the same data:

    feature — one or two considered picks standing as evidence for the point just
              made. Full-width cards carrying the client logo as well as the title,
              because there's room and the job is to make a pick look worth opening.
    marquee — several studies drifting past, so the volume reads at a glance rather
              than as a long scroll.

  WHICH ONE is a question of how many, not only of what was asked. [[casestudies:all]]
  always marquees — the visitor asked for the lot. But a plain [[casestudies]] can also
  come back with a handful (a broad question narrows to the featured picks, and there
  may be five of those), and the feature layout caps at 2-up: five full-width cards at
  a 3:2 well is thousands of pixels of scroll buried inside one answer. Past the
  threshold below it marquees too.

  Skills and summary are deliberately NOT passed through in either view. These are
  discovery cards in the middle of a conversation — enough to decide whether to open
  something, no more. The card component still renders them when given them, for the
  case study page where the detail belongs. The logo goes to the feature only; the
  marquee's cards are too narrow to carry a 260px chip beside the title.
#}
{% set modifier = modifier ?? null %}
{# Normalised first: this file is rendered by the panel registry, which can hand over
   nothing at all, and `|length` on an undefined variable is a Twig error — which
   renderComponent swallows, silently losing the whole panel. #}
{% set caseStudies = caseStudies ?? [] %}
{# Above this many, the feature layout stops reading as a considered pick and starts
   reading as a list. Three is the most that still feels chosen. #}
{% set featureMax = 3 %}
{% set isMarquee = modifier == 'all' or caseStudies|length > featureMax %}

{% if caseStudies %}
    {% if isMarquee %}
        {# One run of cards; clients-marquee.js fills it out, clones it for a
           seamless loop and sets the duration from the run's width. Both variants use
           __items / __item — only the modifier on the block says which layout it is —
           so the extra __track wrapper is the one structural difference. It has to
           exist: the JS writes a transform to it while the block above clips. #}
        {# The label has to follow the reason for the marquee, not the layout: it's
           only "all" when the visitor asked for all. #}
        <div class="c-case-studies c-case-studies--marquee js-case-studies-marquee"
             role="group" aria-label="{{ modifier == 'all' ? 'All case studies' : 'Case studies' }}">
            <div class="c-case-studies__track">
                <ul class="c-case-studies__items">
                    {# Reversed, and only here. The strip drifts left to right, so cards
                       leave at the right edge and arrive at the left one — meaning the
                       card a visitor meets next is the one BEFORE it in this list. Laid
                       out in source order the strip would count backwards through them;
                       reversing the markup puts the intended order back in the reveal.
                       The feature grid below is static and stays as it comes. #}
                    {% for cs in caseStudies|reverse %}
                        {# No logo here. These cards are narrower than a feature card,
                           and a 260px chip beside the text leaves nothing for the
                           title — the client eyebrow already names who it was for. #}
                        {# One title across both variants and the study's own page.
                           (Comments can't live inside the hash literal below —
                           Twig fails to parse it and the whole panel silently
                           disappears.) #}
                        <li class="c-case-studies__item">
                            {% include '_components/case-study' with {
                                url: cs.url,
                                title: cs.title,
                                client: cs.client,
                                image: cs.image ?? null,
                            } only %}
                        </li>
                    {% endfor %}
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    {% else %}
        <div class="c-case-studies c-case-studies--feature">
            <ul class="c-case-studies__items">
                {% for cs in caseStudies %}
                    {# The card is the doorway to the study's page, so the two have to
                       agree — a card and a page headed differently reads as a wrong
                       link. Both come from `longTitle ?: title` via FindContext.

                       Feature cards span the 1110 panel — twice the marquee slide — so
                       they override the component's marquee-sized defaults: 1110 for
                       display, 2220 for 2x, which is what the Thumbnail field asks to
                       be uploaded. (Comments can't go inside the hash below: Twig
                       fails to parse it and the whole panel silently disappears.) #}
                    <li class="c-case-studies__item">
                        {% include '_components/case-study' with {
                            url: cs.url,
                            title: cs.title,
                            client: cs.client,
                            image: cs.image ?? null,
                            logo: cs.logo ?? null,
                            imageWidths: [1110, 2220],
                            imageSizes: '(min-width: 80em) 1110px, 100vw',
                            imageFallback: 1110,
                        } only %}
                    </li>
                {% endfor %}
            </ul>
        </div>
    {% endif %}
{% endif %}
